1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,640
December is the busiest time
of the year to send money home.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:07,440
In just five weeks last festive season,

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,200
South Africa's borders counted more
than four point nine million travellers.

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:16,960
Meet Nyasha. She does hair
at a salon in Johannesburg.

5
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:21,280
Her mother and her two children
live in Masvingo, in Zimbabwe.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:26,080
And every year, around the middle
of December, she does three things.

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:29,120
She gets paid. She sends money home.

8
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,800
And she joins the queue for a bus north.

9
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:38,027
She isn't alone. Millions of people
across southern Africa do the same thing,

10
00:00:38,053 --> 00:00:40,160
in the same few weeks.

11
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,240
So why December? Where
does all that money go?

12
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:48,320
And why does December money hide
a problem that only shows up in

13
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:51,720
January? Let's follow Nyasha home.

14
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,280
First, how big is the December rush?

15
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:59,440
South Africa's government counts every
traveller at its borders over the festive

16
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:05,013
season. From the tenth of December twenty
twenty-five to the fifteenth of January,

17
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:09,680
more than four point nine million travellers
passed through seventy-one ports of

18
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,880
entry. The plan expected people to leave
between the tenth and the thirty-first of

19
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:19,240
December, and come back in the
first two weeks of January.

20
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,733
Beitbridge, the border with Zimbabwe,

21
00:01:21,867 --> 00:01:25,640
handled more than six hundred
and eighteen thousand travellers.

22
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,027
Lebombo, the road to Mozambique,

23
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:31,400
handled more than seven hundred
and fifty-five thousand.

24
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:35,000
And two years ago,
Beitbridge was even busier:

25
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,400
over the twenty
twenty-three festive season,

26
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:41,800
it handled more than seven hundred
and forty-five thousand people.

27
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:44,600
This happens every single year.

28
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,760
Buses packed with suitcases,
blankets and groceries.

29
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:51,160
Taxis loaded to the roof.

30
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,293
Nyasha is somewhere in that queue, with
a bag of school shoes for her children.

31
00:01:56,347 --> 00:02:00,920
And on almost every one of those
journeys, money is moving too.

32
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,680
Cash in envelopes, money transfers,

33
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,000
and airtime and data sent
to a phone back home,

34
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,360
the kind of top-up we do at Fluxr.

35
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:11,320
More on that in a minute.

36
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:15,560
The World Bank put it simply:
as the holiday season approaches,

37
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,840
the flow of remittances intensifies.

38
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:23,840
Remittances means money sent home by
people working in another country.

39
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,120
So why December? There
are three big reasons.

40
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,560
Reason number one: payday comes early.

41
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:35,520
Many South African employers
pay December salaries early.

42
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,280
Standard Bank says some pay
as early as the thirteenth.

43
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:41,800
So for a few days in mid-December,

44
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:45,400
more people have their pay
in hand at the same time.

45
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,960
And many of them, like Nyasha,
send part of it straight home.

46
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,400
Reason number two: January is coming.

47
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,520
In Zimbabwe, schools opened on the
thirteenth of January this year.

48
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:01,080
In Malawi, government
schools opened on the fifth.

49
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:06,880
So the money sent in December often
has a job waiting: uniforms, shoes,

50
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:10,400
books and fees, all before school starts.

51
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,240
Reason number three:
the festive season itself.

52
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:18,800
Christmas food, gifts for the
children, and for many families,

53
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:21,920
the one time of the year
everyone is home together.

54
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,560
People who can't make the
trip send something instead.

55
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:29,760
Airtime and data, so they can
still call home on the day.

56
00:03:30,719 --> 00:03:33,119
So how much money are we talking about?

57
00:03:33,759 --> 00:03:37,599
Take Zimbabwe. According to
the Reserve Bank of Zimbabwe,

58
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:42,799
people living abroad sent home two point
four five billion US dollars in twenty

59
00:03:42,879 --> 00:03:48,359
twenty-five. That was up from about two
point one five billion the year before.

60
00:03:48,719 --> 00:03:54,399
The United Kingdom sent the most, about
seven hundred and ten million dollars.

61
00:03:54,519 --> 00:03:59,119
South Africa was right behind it,
at about seven hundred and three million.

62
00:03:59,279 --> 00:04:03,679
And it's growing. By the end
of August twenty twenty-six,

63
00:04:03,719 --> 00:04:08,959
Zimbabwe had already received about
one point eight six billion dollars.

64
00:04:09,039 --> 00:04:14,239
The finance minister expects more than
three point two billion for the whole year.

65
00:04:14,319 --> 00:04:16,399
It isn't only Zimbabwe.

66
00:04:16,479 --> 00:04:19,039
Look further afield, at the Philippines,

67
00:04:19,119 --> 00:04:22,799
one of the biggest receivers
of remittances in the world.

68
00:04:22,839 --> 00:04:27,359
In December twenty twenty-five,
money sent home hit a record:

69
00:04:27,519 --> 00:04:31,519
three point five two billion
dollars in one month,

70
00:04:31,599 --> 00:04:34,559
twenty-one percent more than in November.

71
00:04:34,679 --> 00:04:37,039
Same story, different country.

72
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:39,972
When the holidays come, people send more.

73
00:04:40,026 --> 00:04:42,039
And for the families waiting at home,

74
00:04:42,079 --> 00:04:47,066
that money is often the difference
between a thin Christmas and a good one.

75
00:04:47,092 --> 00:04:49,572
But there's a catch with December money.

76
00:04:49,626 --> 00:04:52,119
It doesn't show up on any receipt.

77
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:53,359
It shows up in

78
00:04:54,079 --> 00:04:57,172
January. First, a quick word from us.

79
00:04:57,226 --> 00:05:01,919
If you're in South Africa, you can send
airtime and data home with Fluxr.

80
